  "query_preview": "..\n\nLongwave troughing pattern holds in place this morning over eastern \nCONUS, with an embedded shortwave racing across the central Plains. \nHigh based cirrus associated with this wave and convective cloud \ndebris clip the southern Great Lakes, but otherwise surface high \npressure successfully keeps moisture well to our south to support \nplenty of sunshine and dry weather. The complementary thermal trough \nkeeps highs a few degrees cooler than normal today--in the low 80s, \nwith a couple degree boost into the mid-80s Saturday. \n\nDry conditions persist Saturday, but with a pattern shift on its way \nas winds organize out of the southwest during the day. Aloft, mid-\nlevel heights rise modestly in response to ridge amplification over \nwestern CONUS and the southern push of a strengthening Hudson Bay \nlow. This draws the theta-e gradient east, folding instability into \nthe Saginaw Valley and Thumb region early Sunday morning which \nexpands south and east through the day. Multiple waves ripple around \nthe ridge Sunday, attempting to engage the destabilizing airmass and \ngenerate showers and thunderstorms. Timing of these waves is still \nuncertain, although the latest 00z/06z guidance favors a round of \nmorning convection and a second round of convection around peak \nheating. Opted for more bullish PoPs across the Saginaw Valley/Thumb \nSunday morning given the strong synoptic support as the left exit \nregion of an ~85 knot jet clips atop the building instability. \nMUCAPE will still be ramping up though, so only advertising a slight \nchance of thunder with this activity. Lingering dry air and/or \nsubsidence from prior convection will then be hurdles to overcome \nSunday afternoon, both of which could stall the eastward progress of \nmoisture and instability. That said, if deep convection can develop \nin this environment, storms may become organized given the strong \nshear profiles (35-40 knots 0-6 km bulk shear). This is supported by \nthe latest SWODY3 Marginal Risk and CSU machine learning \nprobabilities. \n\nThunderstorm chances continue Sunday night through early next week \nas SE Michigan remains on the edge of the instability gradient under \nan active northwesterly jet and wave train. Best opportunity for \norganized severe weather comes sometime early next week as the low \ncurrently off the coast of the Pacific Northwest releases east \naround the ridge and induces cyclogenesis. Majority of deterministic \nmodels and ensemble members bring this wave to the Great Lakes \nregion Monday night, although a smaller subset delays that until \nTuesday. The strong synoptic signal in the guidance leans toward \norganized severe weather potential. \n\nOutside of thunderstorm chances, the upstream ridge sends heat and \nhumidity into SE Michigan beginning Sunday and lasting until the \nsynoptic cold front comes through mid-week. Forecast highs in the \nupper 80s-low 90s carry low confidence given the thunderstorm \npotential and cloud debris. Heat indices in the mid-90s will be \nattainable if a drier outcome materializes.",
